WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.515 --> 00:00:07.050
SPEAKER: As enterprises
digitally transform themselves,

00:00:07.050 --> 00:00:09.240
networks are at the foundation.

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:10.830
While networks are
constantly modified

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:12.720
to support business
applications,

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:14.310
you need a single
pane of glass that

00:00:14.310 --> 00:00:17.460
can address all aspects of
troubleshooting and monitoring

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:20.100
so that complex connectivity
and network performance

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:22.980
issues are easier to diagnose.

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:26.070
Network Intelligence Center
is Google Cloud's framework

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:29.610
for network monitoring across
the cloud and on premise.

00:00:29.610 --> 00:00:33.030
It improves user experience,
security, and operations

00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:36.030
by providing a comprehensive
platform for monitoring,

00:00:36.030 --> 00:00:39.390
visualizing, and troubleshooting
network health across the cloud

00:00:39.390 --> 00:00:41.270
and data center.

00:00:41.270 --> 00:00:44.850
Network Topology is a module
in Network Intelligence Center

00:00:44.850 --> 00:00:47.910
that helps you visualize your
network so that you can monitor

00:00:47.910 --> 00:00:49.140
your network health easily.

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:54.420
We have heard from customers
that visualizing cloud

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:57.830
topologies is not trivial, as
the cloud network is really

00:00:57.830 --> 00:00:59.030
a mesh.

00:00:59.030 --> 00:01:01.190
Everything talks
to everything else.

00:01:01.190 --> 00:01:04.760
Imagine 1,000 instances
talking among themselves.

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:07.340
There is also a difference
between an on prem and cloud

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:09.560
environment, and it's
challenging to navigate

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:11.480
and understand the differences.

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:13.160
And customers want
to ensure that there

00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:16.190
is no gap between tribal
knowledge of their deployment

00:01:16.190 --> 00:01:17.600
and reality.

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:20.090
So our goal is to
allow you to navigate

00:01:20.090 --> 00:01:21.860
the visualization
of your network

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:24.920
with structure and insights
so that network monitoring

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:27.650
and troubleshooting
becomes easier for you.

00:01:27.650 --> 00:01:30.620
You save time, and in many
cases avoid network issues

00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:32.940
altogether.

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:34.670
Let's start by seeing
a visualization

00:01:34.670 --> 00:01:38.660
of your project, which is your
current deployment within GCP.

00:01:38.660 --> 00:01:41.780
On the top, you can see
all the external clients.

00:01:41.780 --> 00:01:44.880
We have traffic going to three
geographic regions here--

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:48.470
Americas, EMEA, and APAC.

00:01:48.470 --> 00:01:50.390
You can drill down
into any of these

00:01:50.390 --> 00:01:52.670
and see which specific
countries the traffic is

00:01:52.670 --> 00:01:55.370
coming from or being served to.

00:01:55.370 --> 00:01:58.190
In the middle, you
have load balancers.

00:01:58.190 --> 00:02:01.190
Again, you can drill in and
see all the different load

00:02:01.190 --> 00:02:03.530
balancers deployed.

00:02:03.530 --> 00:02:06.650
On the bottom here, you
have your GCE instances

00:02:06.650 --> 00:02:09.229
and the regions that
they're deployed in.

00:02:09.229 --> 00:02:11.420
You can drill down to
the specific VM instance

00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:12.290
level as needed.

00:02:15.090 --> 00:02:17.270
Many customers want to
use this to quickly verify

00:02:17.270 --> 00:02:18.530
certain policies.

00:02:18.530 --> 00:02:20.415
For instance, let's
say you have a policy

00:02:20.415 --> 00:02:22.790
in which all the traffic should
be going through the load

00:02:22.790 --> 00:02:24.170
balancers.

00:02:24.170 --> 00:02:27.710
You can click on the GCE region
or an external geo region

00:02:27.710 --> 00:02:30.933
to see all the traffic
and verify the policy.

00:02:30.933 --> 00:02:33.350
Here you can see that there
is some traffic going directly

00:02:33.350 --> 00:02:36.290
from a GCE instance
to the internet.

00:02:36.290 --> 00:02:38.960
Another example of a common
use case for customers

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:41.960
is to proactively verify if
the traffic is being served out

00:02:41.960 --> 00:02:43.820
of the optimal region.

00:02:43.820 --> 00:02:46.910
For instance, here you can
see that the APAC clients

00:02:46.910 --> 00:02:50.690
are being predominantly served
out of the Asia East region.

00:02:50.690 --> 00:02:53.030
This would help you troubleshoot
performance issues that

00:02:53.030 --> 00:02:55.610
may happen as a result of
traffic being served out

00:02:55.610 --> 00:02:58.340
of a non-optimal region.

00:02:58.340 --> 00:03:00.740
You would be able to easily
see which regions the traffic

00:03:00.740 --> 00:03:02.690
is being served out of.

00:03:02.690 --> 00:03:05.060
You can also see the
evolution of topology

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:06.650
by going back in
time by clicking

00:03:06.650 --> 00:03:09.060
on this timeline on the bottom.

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:11.360
This is very useful
in several scenarios.

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:13.700
For instance, if a
network event happened

00:03:13.700 --> 00:03:16.470
and you want to troubleshoot
it, you can look at the topology

00:03:16.470 --> 00:03:18.590
around the event
using this capability

00:03:18.590 --> 00:03:21.020
to determine any
topology changes you

00:03:21.020 --> 00:03:24.560
may have contributed
to the network issue.

00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:26.570
During migration it
helps you to monitor

00:03:26.570 --> 00:03:29.870
the state of your deployment
as you migrate your workloads,

00:03:29.870 --> 00:03:33.140
thus providing better visibility
into your migration process

00:03:33.140 --> 00:03:34.820
and also letting you
track it by looking

00:03:34.820 --> 00:03:38.120
at the topology
evolution through time.

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:41.700
For troubleshooting, the search
capability is very helpful.

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:44.630
For instance, if you wanted to
search for a specific instance

00:03:44.630 --> 00:03:48.750
and see what all it was talking
to or a specific load balancer,

00:03:48.750 --> 00:03:52.070
you can do all those kinds of
search using this capability.

00:03:52.070 --> 00:03:54.140
That way you can drill
down into exactly what

00:03:54.140 --> 00:03:56.570
you're searching for.

00:03:56.570 --> 00:03:58.580
All edges in the
visualization have metrics

00:03:58.580 --> 00:04:00.450
associated with them.

00:04:00.450 --> 00:04:03.110
You can click on any edge and
view how the metric changed

00:04:03.110 --> 00:04:05.090
over a period of time.

00:04:05.090 --> 00:04:08.000
You can see throughput in these
metrics, ingress and egress

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:12.470
byte counts, and others for
monitoring your network health.

00:04:12.470 --> 00:04:14.530
If you have VPC
Flow Logs available

00:04:14.530 --> 00:04:16.360
and you want to drill
in more to analyze

00:04:16.360 --> 00:04:18.800
those logs, to
make it simple, we

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:20.899
show you the query to
the actual flow logs that

00:04:20.899 --> 00:04:23.090
contributed to this traffic.

00:04:23.090 --> 00:04:25.880
You can click on this link,
which will create the query

00:04:25.880 --> 00:04:28.160
and show you the flow logs.

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:31.190
You could also use some of
these metrics for optimizations.

00:04:31.190 --> 00:04:34.430
For instance, I am only
looking at the regions here

00:04:34.430 --> 00:04:37.100
and how you could do certain
optimizations by understanding

00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:40.600
what's contributing to egress
traffic across regions.

00:04:40.600 --> 00:04:46.580
Here I'm looking at traffic from
US Central 1 to Europe West 1.

00:04:46.580 --> 00:04:49.280
And lastly, you can also
look at your topology

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:52.580
at the organization level
to get an overview of all

00:04:52.580 --> 00:04:54.218
your projects.

00:04:54.218 --> 00:04:56.510
You can see how your projects
are talking to each other

00:04:56.510 --> 00:04:59.810
and verify that it
meets your intent.

00:04:59.810 --> 00:05:00.650
Congratulations.

00:05:00.650 --> 00:05:02.990
You're all set up to start
using the network topology

00:05:02.990 --> 00:05:05.240
module and Network
Intelligence Center

00:05:05.240 --> 00:05:09.410
to visualize, monitor, and
troubleshoot your network.

00:05:09.410 --> 00:05:13.060
[MUSIC PLAYING]

